there are serious downsides to this, its a tradeoff and I wouldn't say they are "better pointers".  There are definitely cases where it can work (e.g. uniform objects that either rapidly create/destroy or all die at a boundary). This is usually the case with a "simple memory model" domain like games, rendering, etc.  All the work starts and ends at a request, frame, or scene.
But for anything else, handles can be trouble.
First, you're obviously paying indirection tax on EVERY lookup.  Logic like `x->foo` becomes `memory_allocator.objects[x->index]->foo`. You're also paying the generation counter tax (assuming you want any kind of safety).  So you also have to do something like this:
if (x->index < 0 || x->index > memory_allocator.objects.size()) { // panic? }
auto *ref = memory_allocator.objects[x->index];
if (!ref) { // panic? }
if (ref->generation != x->generation) { /// panic? }
return *ref;
Next, you have type bloat.  Hopefully you are making type-safe versions of all these indices so you can't just use an aribtrary index into any kind of array right?  Ok well that means now you have double the types (one index type per pointer type).  Ok maybe not the worst, just a lot of boi
